  [PASS] INV-1  I(e)=T_r v0^2/ell is a smooth, well-defined function of the orbit eccentricity e (which grows monotonically from 0 at the circular orbit toward 1); the quadrature converges (endpoint sqrt singularity regularized by the cos-substitution)   (e in [0.221, 0.966], I in [4.652, 50.475])
  [PASS] INV-1b [absolute anchor] as e->0 the radial period is the epicyclic period T_r = 2 pi/kappa with kappa^2 = Phi_eff''(rho_c=1) = 2, so I(e->0) = 2 pi/sqrt(2) = pi*sqrt(2) ~ 4.4429; the quadrature reproduces this closed-form value -- an absolute check, not just internal self-consistency   (I(e=0.0071) = 4.44309 vs 2pi/sqrt(2) = 4.44288)

